# What promoting a concatenation, a comparison, a logical operator or a
# prefix operator has to keep, by kind.
#
# **Shapes**, which ask what the operator answers: every concatenation
# spelling, both comparison families, every logical operator, each of the
# prefix operators `+`, `-` and `\`, and an operand that is itself an operator
# -- including a prefix over a prefix and a prefix over a binary operator's
# result, which is where an operand register holds an intermediate rather than
# a read. An operand that is a **call** is the other way an operand register
# is filled by something other than a read, and it is the one whose op is
# addressed rather than computed: the op carries the route down to the call's
# own node, so the rows put the call on each side of an operator and under a
# prefix, where a route that always stepped the same way lands on the wrong
# node.
#
# **Settings**, which is where a comparison reads state a concatenation does
# not. `NUMERIC DIGITS` rounds the operands before they are compared, so
# narrowing it makes two different numbers equal; `NUMERIC FUZZ` widens that
# further by subtracting from the working precision. A strict comparison reads
# neither -- it compares bytes -- and the rows put the two side by side under
# the same settings so that one row says which family read what.
# The prefix `-` reads `NUMERIC DIGITS` too, and that setting is what says it
# is `0 - x` rather than a sign flip on the operand's own digits. Both forms
# are rows of the same stanza under the same setting, so the file shows that
# agreement rather than citing it from somewhere a reader cannot run.
#
# **Failure**, which for these operators is nearly all in the operands.
# Concatenation renders both operands and cannot itself raise; a comparison
# falls back to a string compare rather than failing when an operand will not
# convert. The raise a binary operator here makes of its own is the logical
# family's 34.901, and its rows pin both halves of the measured rule: **both
# operands are checked even when the first has already decided the answer**,
# and when both are bad it is the **left** operand's text that is reported.
# The prefix operators are the other way round -- the failure is the
# operator's, over its one operand -- and `\` and `-` fail *differently* on
# the same operand: `\` checks text and gives 34.901 where `-` converts and
# gives 41.1, so the rows put `'abc'` under each.
# A call operand adds places a failure can come from that a read cannot: from
# inside the call's own argument list, from the callee, and from the operator
# over the value the call answered. The rows separate them by condition
# number, each under a `SIGNAL ON SYNTAX` of its own.
#
# **Trace**, which is where a native op diverges first and where no other
# instrument in this crate can see it. `eval.rs` emits a binary operator's
# `>O>` line and a prefix operator's `>P>` line as a side effect of evaluating
# them, and a call's `>F>` line the same way; an op that only computes into
# a register emits nothing, and every line after the missing one still matches
# -- so only an exact stderr comparison catches it. Both directions are here:
# a `trace i` row requires the lines, and a `trace r` row requires their
# absence. What a nested call adds is a *position*: its `>F>` sits between the
# `>A>` of its own argument and the `>O>` or `>P>` of the operator holding it,
# which is a place only an operator around the call can put it.
# The `trace i` row for the binary operators carries the abuttal, whose tag is
# the empty string, and the blank, whose tag is one space. Neither has any
# source text under it, so an implementation reading a spelling off the source
# span gets both wrong, and no other row can see that. **The prefix `\` has no
# row of that shape and cannot have one here.** What would exercise it is a `\`
# written `0xAA` or `0xAC`: measured, the oracle tags `\`, `0xAA` and `0xAC`
# alike as `>P>   "\"`, so for the non-ASCII pair the canonical spelling and
# the source bytes differ. But neither byte is valid UTF-8, and both the
# program and the `*-*` echo in its expected block would carry one, while
# `datadriven` reads this file through `fs::read_to_string`. Every `\` below is
# the ASCII one, whose span and canonical spelling are the same bytes.
#
# **A prefix operator's line is `>P>` and never `>O>`**, and
# `tests/trace_oracle`'s `prefix_operators` witness already pins those bytes on
# the **compiled** engine: `check_witness` runs `Invocation::none()`, whose
# engine is `Engine::DEFAULT`, which is `Engine::Ir`. What the `trace i` prefix
# row adds is the other arm -- the tree-walker's own bytes, and the two engines
# agreeing on them line for line, which `render_both_engines` asserts before
# the expected block below is compared at all.
#
# **What this file is worth was measured rather than assumed, and it is not
# mutation-catching.** Four mutations of the promoted binary path were each
# re-run with this file moved out of the directory, and every one was caught
# anyway:
# dropping the `>O>` echo behind the new op reddens `calls`, whose own row
# holds a blank operator; checking a logical operator's right operand before
# its left reddens a unit test written for that rule; a comparison that
# ignores `NUMERIC FUZZ` reddens another; and `|` computing exclusive-or --
# the one the operand-combination rows below were written for, because this
# file's own logical stanza could not see it before them -- reddens
# `assertions.rs`'s `the_exempt_set_matches_the_current_blocked_rows`, which
# runs rows extracted from ooTest rather than anything written here: seven of
# those rows stop passing, so the not-passing set no longer matches the
# committed exempt list and that test's own equality fails.
#
# The row written to close that blind spot did not make the file a sole
# catcher even for the mutation it closes -- and these rows do not have to be
# sole catchers to earn their place.
#
# **So the reason to keep these rows is that they pin oracle bytes, which is
# something an engine-against-engine comparison structurally cannot do**: both
# arms of that comparison are this crate, so a shared path that is wrong the
# same way twice passes it, and a unit test says what this crate should print
# rather than what the C++ interpreter does.
#
# EVERY EXPECTED BYTE BELOW WAS MEASURED AGAINST THE C++ ORACLE, and every row
# was measured to produce the identical bytes on both engines before these
# operators were promoted as well as after -- which is what says a row can
# tell whether it would ever have failed. Regenerate a row by running its
# program under the oracle, never with REWRITE=1 -- ir_recorded.rs refuses to run
# at all under REWRITE for that reason. Capture is:
#
#   ( ulimit -v 1048576; LD_LIBRARY_PATH=<ooRexx>/build/lib \
#     <ooRexx>/build/bin/rexx PROGRAM.rex </dev/null )
#
# run from a fresh empty directory: the scratchpad is on the oracle's
# external-routine search path and a program calling an unresolved name finds
# a stale file there and runs it.
#
# Expected lines are tagged so that no line of the block can be empty: an empty
# line ends an expected block, and a program's own output may hold one.
# Trailing blanks are part of what is compared.

# every concatenation spelling, over the same two operands: `||`, the
# abuttal between two terms with nothing between them, and the blank between
# two terms with whitespace. **The blank inserts exactly one space however much
# whitespace separated the terms** -- `say za zb` is written twice, once with
# one space and once with four, and the two answer identically, because the
# scanner has collapsed that distinction before the parser sees it.
# The last rows chain the spellings, which is what makes one join's
# result the next one's operand register.
program
za = 'p'
zb = 'q'
say za || zb
say za'r'
say za zb
say za    zb
say 'a' || za || zb
say za || zb 'r'
say za'r'zb
----
rc> 0
out> pq
out> pr
out> p q
out> p q
out> apq
out> pq r
out> prq

# both comparison families over the same operands. The non-strict one
# compares numerically when both sides convert (`'1' = '1.0'`) and drops to a
# string compare when either does not (`'ABC' < 'abd'`); the strict one
# compares bytes always, so it separates `1` from `1.0` and keeps the blank in
# `' 1'` that the numeric compare ignores.
program
za = '1'
zb = '1.0'
say za = zb
say za == zb
say ' 1' = 1
say ' 1' == 1
say 'ABC' < 'abd'
say 'ABC' >> 'abd'
say za \= zb
say za \== zb
say 2 <> 3
say 2 >< 2
----
rc> 0
out> 1
out> 0
out> 1
out> 0
out> 1
out> 0
out> 0
out> 1
out> 1
out> 0

# NUMERIC DIGITS narrowed, which a comparison reads and a concatenation does
# not: the operands are rounded to the precision before they are compared, so
# at DIGITS 4 `1.0001` and `1` are equal and at DIGITS 9 they are not. **The
# strict rows are the control and there is one at each setting**, which is what
# says the setting cannot move a byte comparison -- a single strict row would
# only have said the families differ where it sat.
program
numeric digits 4
say 1.0001 = 1
say 1.001 = 1
say 1.0001 == 1
numeric digits 9
say 1.0001 = 1
say 1.0001 == 1
----
rc> 0
out> 1
out> 0
out> 0
out> 0
out> 0

# NUMERIC FUZZ, the other setting a comparison reads: it subtracts from the
# precision the comparison works at, so at FUZZ 3 `1000000.0` and `1000001`
# compare equal, differing in the seventh significant digit, while `1000000.0`
# and `1000100`, differing in the fifth, do not. FUZZ then goes back to zero at
# the same site, so the rows say the setting is read when the comparison runs
# rather than when the clause was compiled. **The strict rows are the control
# and there is one at each setting**, for the reason the DIGITS stanza above
# gives.
#
# **The second row is the other control, and the reason the first is spelled
# with a decimal point.** Two integer objects inside DIGITS are compared by
# `RexxInteger::comp` (`interpreter/classes/IntegerClass.cpp:1191`), which
# subtracts them directly and never reads FUZZ, so `1000000 = 1000001` answers
# `0` at every FUZZ and demonstrates nothing about the setting. This stanza
# asserted the opposite until 2026-08-19 -- expecting `1` there, which no
# interpreter has ever printed -- so it was recording this crate's own answer
# rather than the oracle's, against this file's own header rule. `numeric-fuzz`
# beside it is where that path is covered in both directions.
program
numeric fuzz 3
say 1000000.0 = 1000001
say 1000000 = 1000001
say 1000000.0 == 1000001
say 1000000.0 = 1000100
numeric fuzz 0
say 1000000.0 = 1000001
say 1000000.0 == 1000001
----
rc> 0
out> 1
out> 1
out> 0
out> 0
out> 0
out> 0

